msgid "methodology.heading"
msgstr "How we grade"

msgid "methodology.title"
msgstr "How the disclosure grades are calculated"

msgid "methodology.description"
msgstr ""
"Every credible range, the reasoning behind it, how suppressed and not-applicable fields leave "
"the denominator, and why an ungradeable institution gets no grade rather than a zero."

msgid "methodology.lede"
msgstr ""
"Every credible range here is a judgement call, so every one carries a written rationale. A "
"scorecard that cannot be disputed line by line is not a scorecard, it is an accusation."

msgid "methodology.measured.heading"
msgstr "What is being measured"

msgid "methodology.measured.body"
msgstr ""
"Not quality. The grade answers one question: of the things this institution was in a position "
"to tell the public, how much did it actually tell them? A well-funded university with poor "
"outcomes that reports every field completely scores higher here than a good school that "
"reports nothing. Outcomes are graded elsewhere, by other people, using the very data this "
"project is checking the existence of."

msgid "methodology.absent.heading"
msgstr "How an absent value is classified"

msgid "methodology.absent.body"
msgstr ""
"A field an institution did not report, a field suppressed to protect a small cohort, and a "
"field whose true value is zero are three different facts. Rendered carelessly they all become "
"<code>0</code> on a page, and a reader cannot tell a college that admits nobody from a college "
"that declined to say. Every value is classified before anything else touches it."

msgid "methodology.absent.caption"
msgstr ""
"The five ways a value can be absent or present, and which of them an institution is answerable "
"for."

msgid "methodology.absent.column.classification"
msgstr "Classification"

msgid "methodology.absent.column.meaning"
msgstr "Meaning"

msgid "methodology.absent.column.counts"
msgstr "Counts against the institution?"

msgid "methodology.counts_against.yes"
msgstr "yes"

msgid "methodology.counts_against.no"
msgstr "no"

msgid "methodology.absent.suppression"
msgstr ""
"Suppression is a policy decision made for good reasons and is never held against anyone. "
"Penalising an institution for protecting a twelve-person cohort would push publishers toward "
"disclosing things they should not, which is the opposite of the point. Suppressed and "
"not-applicable fields leave the denominator rather than scoring zero, and an institution with "
"an empty denominator gets <strong>no grade at all</strong>, not a zero."

msgid "methodology.published_failure.heading"
msgstr "Why a published value can still be a failure"

msgid "methodology.published_failure.body"
msgstr ""
"A value being present is not sufficient; it also has to be credible for the field it sits in. "
"A published zero survives in federal data because zero is a legal number, not because anyone "
"measured it. A wrong number published as fact is worse than a gap, because the gap is visible "
"to a reader and the wrong number is not."

msgid "methodology.peers.heading"
msgstr "Peer comparison"

msgid "methodology.peers.rule"
msgstr ""
"A fixed credible range is a blunt instrument and an institution can reasonably object to one. "
"So every implausible finding carries its peer group: sector, level, and state, with the "
"institution excluded from its own comparison. A reader who thinks a grade is unfair can see "
"what comparable institutions published and attack the peer definition, the sample, or the "
"conclusion. All three are better arguments to be having than one about where a constant was "
"set."

msgid "methodology.peers.against_itself"
msgstr ""
"Where the peers turn out to publish the same value, the verdict says so and the finding argues "
"against itself. That is intended and is not suppressed."

msgid "methodology.peers.both_counts"
msgstr ""
"A peer claim is only made where at least {min_peers} comparable institutions exist <em>and</em> "
"at least {min_peers} of them published the field. Both counts, because the second is what the "
"comparison is made out of: a group of fifty in which six published a number supports a claim "
"about six, and the finding says so instead of borrowing the confidence of the fifty. Below "
"either bar the finding is still reported and still links here; it simply arrives without a "
"peer comparison attached, because an unsupported comparison is worse than none."

msgid "methodology.bands.heading"
msgstr "Letter bands"

msgid "methodology.band.top"
msgstr "{letter}, {threshold} and above."

msgid "methodology.band.next"
msgstr "{letter}, {threshold}."

msgid "methodology.band.bottom"
msgstr "{letter} below that."

msgid "methodology.bands.body"
msgstr ""
"{bands} Fixed rather than curved, because grading on a curve would hide a field-wide collapse "
"in reporting: if everyone stopped publishing graduation rates tomorrow, a curve would report "
"that as normal."

msgid "methodology.drift.heading"
msgstr "Drift"

msgid "methodology.drift.why_two_runs"
msgstr ""
"A single snapshot cannot distinguish a field that was never collected from one that was "
"collected until recently and then stopped. The first is a gap in the data model; the second is "
"a change in what the public is allowed to know. Only the comparison between runs can tell them "
"apart, so per-field counts are committed to version control on every run."

msgid "methodology.drift.rate_not_count"
msgstr ""
"<strong>Drift is a change in rate, not a change in count.</strong> Every comparison divides by "
"the institutions the field applied to in that run, and the reason is a mistake this project "
"made and published to itself. Measured on counts, three IPEDS collection years produced three "
"confident systemic findings and all three were false: between 2021 and 2023 the directory "
"shrank from 6,289 institutions to 6,163, so 130 fewer published a web address, and that was "
"reported as a systemic 2.1% collapse. The share publishing one had gone <em>up</em>, from "
"99.93% to 99.95%. Colleges closed; they did not stop reporting. The one real movement in the "
"period, the athletics disclosure rising from 57.1% to 59.4%, ranked fourth and was never "
"flagged, because 52 is a small number next to 130."

msgid "methodology.drift.threshold"
msgstr ""
"A change is called systemic when that rate moves by at least {systemic} percentage points. The "
"threshold is a judgement call, stated here so a reader can disagree with it, and set low "
"because a coordinated stop-reporting event is newsworthy well before it touches a majority of "
"institutions. Three real collection years say it is roughly right: every year-on-year movement "
"in these six disclosures sits under one point except the athletics disclosure, which rose 1.75 "
"points in a year and 2.26 across two. At {systemic}% the bar flags that and nothing else. At "
"1% it would report ordinary annual churn as policy; at 5% it would have found nothing in three "
"years of federal data, which is not a measurement but a way of never having to say anything."

msgid "methodology.drift.both_directions"
msgstr ""
"Drift is reported in both directions: fields that <em>started</em> being reported are as real "
"a finding as fields that stopped, and reporting only the losses would make this an argument "
"rather than a measurement. A field whose rate cannot be computed in either run is reported as "
"unmeasured and is never called systemic, because an unknown is not a large movement."

msgid "methodology.field.address_only"
msgstr ""
"Graded on whether an address was published, not on what is behind it; no page is ever fetched."

msgid "methodology.field.applies_when"
msgstr ""
" Institutions the requirement does not reach leave the denominator entirely rather than being "
"marked down."

msgid "methodology.field.credible_range"
msgstr "Credible range: {minimum} to {maximum}. An exact zero is {zero}."

msgid "methodology.field.zero_credible"
msgstr "a credible measurement"

msgid "methodology.field.zero_artifact"
msgstr "treated as an artifact"

msgid "methodology.field.weight"
msgstr "Weight {weight}."

msgid "methodology.fields.scorecard.heading"
msgstr "The fields: College Scorecard"

msgid "methodology.fields.ipeds.heading"
msgstr "The fields: IPEDS"

msgid "methodology.fields.ipeds.three_absences"
msgstr ""
"IPEDS records public disclosures the Scorecard does not carry, and states absence three "
"different ways, all of them negative integers: -1 not reported, -2 not applicable, -3 not "
"available. They are not interchangeable, and only the first counts against an institution."

msgid "methodology.fields.ipeds.athletics"
msgstr ""
"The athletics disclosure below was ungraded until a second IPEDS file arrived, and the reason "
"is worth stating. It is blank for 4,469 of 6,163 directory rows, and almost every one of those "
"is a college with no athletics programme, so grading the column against the directory alone "
"would have produced four thousand confident and entirely fabricated violations. The "
"institutional characteristics file carries each institution's own answer about whether it "
"competes, and that answer moves the denominator from 6,163 to 1,998. The finding did not need "
"a better threshold. It needed to know who the rule applied to."

msgid "methodology.fields.ipeds.veterans"
msgstr ""
"One candidate is still deliberately not graded. The veterans information page is blank for "
"2,377 institutions, and the same characteristics file would now supply an applicability rule "
"for it. It stays ungraded because applicability was never the obstacle here: no universal "
"requirement obliges an institution to publish a veterans page, so a rule about who it applied "
"to would be a rule about a duty that does not exist. Knowing who would owe a disclosure is not "
"the same as there being one to owe."

msgid "methodology.wrong.heading"
msgstr "If this is wrong about your institution"

msgid "methodology.wrong.body"
msgstr ""
"The rules above are the whole of it; there is no model anywhere in the grading path and no "
"judgement that is not written down on this page. If a field is marked wrongly, the "
"disagreement will be with a stated bound, a stated peer group, or the underlying federal "
"record, and all three can be checked."

msgid "national.heading"
msgstr "The national picture"

msgid "national.title"
msgstr "What US colleges disclose, nationally"

msgid "national.description"
msgstr ""
"Per-field disclosure counts across every institution in the IPEDS directory, with the "
"applicability rule behind each and the institutions named where a statute requires the "
"disclosure."

msgid "national.why_this_page_differs"
msgstr ""
"Everything else on this site is graded from a sample of the College Scorecard and says so. "
"This page is different: IPEDS publishes its directory as a file rather than as a paged API, so "
"grading it grades every institution there is, and the percentages below describe the country."

msgid "national.discloses.heading"
msgstr "What the country discloses"

msgid "national.table.caption"
msgstr ""
"Per-field national counts. Suppressed and inapplicable institutions are outside the applicable "
"column, never scored as failures."

msgid "national.table.disclosure"
msgstr "Disclosure"

msgid "national.table.reaches"
msgstr "Institutions it reaches"

msgid "national.table.record_carries_none"
msgstr "Record carries none"

msgid "national.table.published"
msgstr "Published"

msgid "national.table.requirement"
msgstr "Requirement"

msgid "national.no_statute"
msgstr "no statute"

#. Both halves of one sentence that begins "The middle column is the whole argument." The
#. reaches are read off the table rather than typed into the prose, because they move every
#. collection year.
msgid "national.middle_column.with_reach"
msgstr ""
"The middle column is the whole argument. A disclosure that reaches {narrowest} institutions "
"and a disclosure that reaches {widest} produce very different-looking failure counts from the "
"same underlying behaviour, and a table that showed only the failures would rank them wrongly."

msgid "national.middle_column.without_reach"
msgstr ""
"The middle column is the whole argument. Two disclosures that reach different numbers of "
"institutions produce very different-looking failure counts from the same underlying behaviour, "
"and a table that showed only the failures would rank them wrongly."

msgid "national.ungradeable_note"
msgid_plural "national.ungradeable_note"
msgstr[0] ""
"{rows} directory row gets no grade at all rather than a zero, because every field checked was "
"either suppressed or outside the reach of the rule. Most are system offices and closed "
"institutions. They are counted here and excluded from every mean."
msgstr[1] ""
"{rows} directory rows get no grade at all rather than a zero, because every field checked was "
"either suppressed or outside the reach of the rule. Most are system offices and closed "
"institutions. They are counted here and excluded from every mean."

msgid "national.named_findings.heading"
msgstr "Named findings"

msgid "national.no_named_findings"
msgstr "No statute-backed disclosure is absent anywhere in this run."

msgid "national.gap.heading"
msgstr "{field}: {count} of {applicable} institutions"

msgid "national.gap.body"
msgstr ""
"Required by {statute}. These are the institutions the requirement reaches for which the "
"federal record carries no address. Named rather than counted because there is a published rule "
"behind this one; the fields with no statute behind them are counted above and their "
"institutions are not listed."

msgid "national.gap.no_unit_id"
msgstr "no unit id published"

msgid "national.caveat"
msgstr ""
"An absent address means the federal record carries none. It is not proof the institution has "
"nothing: it may have published the thing and not reported where. Which of those two is true is "
"not something a blank cell can settle, and this page does not pretend otherwise. The "
"<a href=\"{methodology}\">methodology</a> states the rule behind every row."

msgid "census.heading"
msgstr "The College Scorecard census"

msgid "census.title"
msgstr "The College Scorecard census, in full"

msgid "census.description"
msgstr ""
"Every institution the College Scorecard publishes, graded the same way as the "
"600-institution sample, with both frames' composition stated side by side."

msgid "census.why_this_page_differs"
msgstr ""
"The home page's figures are graded from a 600-institution sample and say so. This page grades "
"every institution the College Scorecard publishes -- the API paged to exhaustion, proven from "
"the walk's own counts rather than assumed from how large the result looks -- and does not "
"replace the sample figures, because the sample is a real, separately-interesting slice and "
"silently swapping one number for another is the failure this project exists to name in other "
"people's data."

msgid "census.headline"
msgstr ""
"In the full census, <strong>{missing} of {applicable}, or {share}, publish no admission rate "
"at all</strong> -- the same question the home page asks of the 600-institution sample, asked "
"here of every institution the Scorecard publishes."

msgid "census.composition.heading"
msgstr "Composition: how skewed was the sample"

msgid "census.composition.body"
msgstr ""
"{ca_sample} of the sample's {sample_total} institutions ({ca_sample_share}) are Californian, "
"across {sample_states} states. The census, {census_total} institutions across {census_states} "
"states, puts California at {ca_census_share}."

msgid "census.sector_table.caption"
msgstr "Institutions by sector, sample against census."

msgid "census.sector_table.sector"
msgstr "Sector"

msgid "census.sector_table.sample"
msgstr "Sample"

msgid "census.sector_table.sample_share"
msgstr "Sample share"

msgid "census.sector_table.census"
msgstr "Census"

msgid "census.sector_table.census_share"
msgstr "Census share"

msgid "census.discloses.heading"
msgstr "What the census discloses"

msgid "census.table.caption"
msgstr ""
"Per-field counts across every institution the Scorecard publishes. Suppressed and inapplicable "
"institutions are outside the applicable column, never scored as failures."

msgid "census.caveat"
msgstr ""
"A field with no statute behind it is this project's opinion about what a college ought to "
"publish, not a legal requirement; the <a href=\"{methodology}\">methodology</a> states the "
"rationale behind every row. Institutions are counted here and not named, the same rule the "
"sample figures on the home page follow."

#. The home page title deliberately omits the site's name: shell.title appends it, and
#. "disclosed: what US colleges do not tell you | disclosed" says it twice.
msgid "home.title"
msgstr "What US colleges do not tell you"

msgid "home.description"
msgstr ""
"Grades US higher-education institutions on what they disclose rather than on how they perform. "
"Which fields go unreported, which published values are not measurements, and what stopped "
"being published."

msgid "home.lede"
msgstr ""
"Grades US higher-education institutions on what they disclose, not on how they perform."

msgid "home.what_this_grades"
msgstr ""
"Plenty of tools will tell you a college's graduation rate. None will tell you how many "
"colleges never reported one, or which fields quietly stopped being published this year. That "
"is what this grades."

msgid "home.why_it_matters"
msgstr ""
"The distinction matters because the two failures look identical on a page. A college with a 0% "
"admission rate and a college that never reported an admission rate both render as a blank or a "
"zero in most tools, and a reader cannot tell them apart."

msgid "home.found.heading"
msgstr "What this run found"

msgid "home.found.graded"
msgstr "Institutions graded"

msgid "home.found.mean"
msgstr "Mean disclosure"

msgid "home.found.not_gradeable"
msgstr "Not gradeable"

msgid "home.found.not_measurements"
msgstr "Published values that are not measurements"

msgid "home.ungradeable_note"
msgid_plural "home.ungradeable_note"
msgstr[0] ""
"{count} institution could not be graded at all, because every field checked was suppressed or "
"inapplicable. It gets no grade rather than a zero, and it is counted separately from the mean "
"so it cannot pass as an institution that scored well."
msgstr[1] ""
"{count} institutions could not be graded at all, because every field checked was suppressed or "
"inapplicable. They get no grade rather than a zero, and they are counted separately from the "
"mean so they cannot pass as institutions that scored well."

msgid "home.worst.heading"
msgstr "Least-reported fields"

msgid "home.worst.caption"
msgstr ""
"Fields most often absent across the institutions in this run. Shares are of this run, not of "
"the country."

msgid "home.worst.field"
msgstr "Field"

msgid "home.worst.not_reporting"
msgstr "Institutions not reporting it"

msgid "home.worst.share"
msgstr "Share"

msgid "home.zeros.heading"
msgstr "Published zeros that are not zeros"

msgid "home.zeros.body"
msgstr ""
"These institutions published a value rather than leaving a gap, and the value is not a "
"plausible measurement of the thing it claims to measure. Each carries the peer group that "
"supports or undermines the finding."

msgid "home.artifact"
msgstr "{institution} publishes {field} as <code>{value}</code>"

msgid "home.by_state.heading"
msgstr "By state"

msgid "home.how.heading"
msgstr "How this works"

msgid "home.how.body"
msgstr ""
"Read the <a href=\"{methodology}\">methodology</a>: every credible range, the reasoning behind "
"it, and why a suppressed field is never held against an institution."

msgid "home.coverage.label"
msgstr "What this run covers."

msgid "home.coverage.no_scope"
msgstr ""
"This report predates the coverage record and does not say how much of the College Scorecard it "
"holds. Treat every percentage on this page as describing the institutions listed here and "
"nothing wider, because nothing wider has been established."

msgid "home.coverage.not_coy"
msgstr ""
"A project about undisclosed information should not be coy about the limits of its own sample."

msgid "home.coverage.national_pointer"
msgstr ""
"The <a href=\"{national}\">national page</a> carries the figures that do describe the country, "
"drawn from a source published as a whole file rather than as a paged API."

msgid "home.coverage.census_pointer"
msgstr ""
"The <a href=\"{census}\">Scorecard census page</a> asks the same questions of every institution "
"the College Scorecard publishes, not this sample, and states both frames' composition side by "
"side."
